// COMPILER BUG (CDZ0201, mis-reported) — filed by v-iterators 2026-07-18, blocks the operator's
// monomorphic/generic/fused iterator redesign (the adapter-record `{state, step}` shape IS this variant).
//
// SYMPTOM: a 2-field sum variant `Mk(s, s -> Option((a, s)))` — a plain state field `s` PLUS an arrow
// payload whose type references BOTH type params (`s` and `a`) — CANNOT BE CONSTRUCTED. Every
// `Mk(x, f)` call reports CDZ0201 "a nullary variant takes the unit value, but a payload of type
// (-> ... (Option (Tuple ... ...))) was applied" — i.e. the constructor is treated as NULLARY, as if
// `Mk` has no declared payload, even though the `type` decl clearly gives it two fields.
//
// The DECL itself type-checks (see CONTROL A). It is CONSTRUCTION that fails.
//
// DISCRIMINATORS (all minimized + verified on trunk ~265496869):
//   - `Mk(s, a)` (2 params, NON-arrow 2nd field) constructs fine.                        [works]
//   - `Mk(s, s -> a)` / `Mk(s -> Option(s))` / `Mk(s -> Option((s,a)))` (arrow, but NOT the
//     state+arrow-over-both-params combo) construct fine.                                [works]
//   - `Mk(s, s -> Option((a, s)))` bare DECL alone (no constructor) type-checks.         [works]
//   - CONSTRUCTING it fails whether the arrow arg is: an inline closure, an inline closure with a
//     FULLY-ANNOTATED param (`fn(s: List(Int64)) => ...` — error then shows the fully-resolved type
//     `(-> (List Int64) (Option (Tuple Int64 (List Int64))))`, still "nullary"), OR a NAMED top-level
//     function passed by name.                                                            [ALL fail]
//   So it is NOT an inference/element-tie gap (the type is fully known) — the constructor simply is not
//   matching a closure/arrow-typed argument against its declared arrow-payload slot when that slot's
//   type mentions two type params behind a `state` field. Likely a variant-payload-arity / arrow-slot
//   resolution bug at construction, NOT a check-time reject (routes to v-inference / rcdzc resolve+lower).
//
// WHY IT MATTERS: this is the EXACT shape of the operator-directed fused-iterator redesign — an iterator
// is `Mk(state, step: state -> Option (elem, state))`. Until a 2-field state+stepper variant can be
// constructed, the adapter-record encoding cannot be built at all. This is the I1-spike blocker.

// CONTROL A — the DECL alone type-checks (no error):
type Ctl = | Mk(s, s -> Option((a, s)))

// THE BUG — constructing it (named fn arg; inline closure + annotated closure fail identically):
type Iter = | Mk(s, s -> Option((a, s)))
def popper(s) = match s with
  | [] => Option.None(unit)
  | [h, .. t] => Option.Some((h, t))
def from-list(xs) = Iter.Mk(xs, popper)   // <-- CDZ0201 "nullary variant takes the unit value" HERE

@test
def constructs-and-steps() =
  match from-list([1, 2, 3]) with
  | Iter.Mk(s0, step) => (match step(s0) with
    | Option.None(_) => trap("empty")
    | Option.Some(p) => (match p with | (h, _rest) => if h == 1 then unit else trap("head")))

// ===== TRIAGE (corpus-bugfix, 2026-07-20, trunk base >= finding era 265496869) =====
// RESOLVED — the 2-field variant Mk(s, s -> Option((a, s))) (state + arrow-over-BOTH-typarams) now
// CONSTRUCTS + STEPS correctly; no CDZ0201 "nullary variant takes the unit value". Verified BOTH forms:
//   - named top-level fn arg (Iter.Mk(xs, popper))               -> compiles, run -> 1 (head of [1,2,3])
//   - inline closure arg (Iter.Mk(xs, fn(s) => match s ...))     -> compiles, run -> 1
// The variant-payload-arity / arrow-slot resolution at construction was fixed between the finding era and
// now. This was v-iterators' I1-SPIKE BLOCKER: the fused-iterator adapter-record IS exactly this
// Mk(state, step: state -> Option(elem, state)) shape — now buildable. Marked RESOLVED (v-inference lane).
